nd for me uh i wanted to take  that question and turn it around and say  well what what can i do to help people  learn a language in the time that they  do have because i think we all we all  have time in our lives that we're  perhaps doing other things that we could  be learning the language while we're  doing those other things right i'm  thinking of things like um in the car or  at the gym absolutely walking the dog or  doing the dishes or whatever whatever  happens to be the thing and i'm not  saying you you use every moment of your  your life your spare time your down time  to learn a language but i think there  are times that we could use for language  learning that we we perhaps waste  and so back in 2006 we started  developing coffee break spanish as a as  a resource that would allow people to  use those 20-minute sessions that they  they may have right constructively for  for learning a language and since then  we've developed uh courses from an a1  level up to almost c1 right in our in  french and spanish and with italian and  german we've got two levels and uh we  just introduced mandarin this year oh  okay  you know i should say that when i go to  learn language typically i use a variety  of resources  and i end up liking some better than  others  for example for my recent efforts at  hebrew and greek i bought living  language which i have to confess that i  found not very good at all  but  we do use different resources and there  are people who come to link and they say  there's no explanation and i don't  understand the grammar and so on and so  on and so i think there is definitely  not only with  coffee break languages but with the  other services that are out there  there's always an element of of a sort  of complementary relationship and so i  think in that sense uh people who come  to link you know i could see people  spending some time with with a resource  like yours or a pimpler or a michelle  thomas and i i think  to the extent that i'm you know familiar  with your product there's more  explanation there's more english it's an  easing into you're sitting in the car  it's not like solid target language and  you don't know what they're talking  about so to that extent it's kind of an  easing into say link which is pure  target language yeah yeah i think i  firmly believe there is there is space  for everyone in the  in the business right um and i think any  anyone using only one resource for  learning a language is crazy because  you do need those different resources  right i mean again i'm all about the  concept of coffee break learning and  being able to to do something for 20  minutes but you know equally you could  spend  an hour doing lessons or indeed if  you've only got the five minutes while  you're waiting for the bus right then  you would need something else to get  your kind of espresso break right  and in that situation yeah whether it's  link whether it's getting an app or  something like that that's filling that  down time with an opportunity to to  practice what you've learned to  consolidate what you've learned in a  different way and getting different  input and and then practicing that with  with uh with the output that you're  using absolutely and i and i think too  that that variety i think you know i  have mentioned in my videos this the  german  neuroscientist who said that the brain  requires  novelty and repetition  and i think variety introduces that's  kind of a way to get the same content  from different sources and different  methods so you don't end up feeling  frustrated that you know you're always  doing the same thing so um yeah i think  you know we're going to see whether  there aren't some ways that  there we can introduce some  complimentary anyway yeah thank you for  the interview  and i think it's a real treat for  because a lot of my listeners are not  native speakers of english and i think  it's the first time  it's the first time we've had some good  scottish english